--TEST--
`Core\Reflect\ParameterInfo::type` answers the type the declaration spells, whole — the nullable, the generic argument and the class name included — `rule:core-classes/reflect`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A parameter's declared type is part of the program's shape, so it is
// introspection and reaches no value. What makes it worth asking for is that a
// *representation* cannot answer it: `int` and `uint` share one, `?int` and
// `int` share one, and every class shares one with every other, so only the
// declaration's own words tell them apart.
class Say {
    public static function text(?string $value): string {
        if ($value == null) { return "<none>"; }
        return $value;
    }
}

class Orders {
    public function place(
        string $sku,
        ?int $quantity,
        array<string> $tags,
        Core\Uuid $id,
    ): string {
        return $sku;
    }
}

var $described = Core\Reflect::forObject(new Orders());

foreach ($described->methods() as Core\Reflect\MethodInfo $method) {
    foreach ($method->parameters() as Core\Reflect\ParameterInfo $parameter) {
        echo $parameter->name(), ": ", Say::text($parameter->type()), "\n";
    }
}
--EXPECT--
sku: string
quantity: ?int
tags: array<string>
id: Core\Uuid
